# Portfolio Contact API

A tiny Express server with one job: take a submission from your portfolio's
contact form and email it to you. Built with Nodemailer, so it works with
any SMTP provider (Gmail, Outlook, Zoho, a custom domain inbox, etc.).

## 1. Install dependencies

```bash
cd backend
npm install
```

## 2. Configure your email credentials

```bash
cp .env.example .env
```

Then open `.env` and fill in your real values.

**Using Gmail (easiest to start with):**
1. Turn on 2-Step Verification on the Google account: https://myaccount.google.com/security
2. Create an App Password: https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords
3. Use that 16-character password as `SMTP_PASS` (not your normal Gmail password)
4. `SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com`, `SMTP_PORT=587`, `SMTP_SECURE=false`

**Using another provider:** any SMTP host/port/credentials work — check your
email provider's SMTP settings page (Outlook, Zoho, and most hosting panels
all publish these).

## 3. Run it locally

```bash
npm start
```

You should see:
```
Contact API running on http://localhost:5000
```

Test it directly:
```bash
curl -X POST http://localhost:5000/api/contact \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"name":"Test User","email":"test@example.com","message":"Hello, just testing!"}'
```

If it worked, you'll get `{"ok":true}` back and an email in your inbox.

## 4. Point the frontend at it

In `assets/main.js`, find the `API_URL` constant near the contact-form
handler and set it to wherever this server is running:

```js
const API_URL = 'http://localhost:5000/api/contact'; // local testing
// const API_URL = 'https://your-deployed-api.com/api/contact'; // production
```

The form falls back to opening the visitor's email client if the API call
fails (e.g. the backend isn't deployed yet), so the site still works either way.

## 5. Deploy it somewhere it can stay running

This is a normal persistent Node/Express server, so it needs a host that
keeps a process running (not a static site host). Good free/cheap options:

- **Render** (render.com) — free tier, connect your GitHub repo, set the
  environment variables in the dashboard, done.
- **Railway** (railway.app) — similar flow, generous free tier.
- **Fly.io** — a bit more setup, more control.

Whichever you use:
1. Push this `backend/` folder to its own GitHub repo (or a subfolder of your main one)
2. Set the same variables from `.env` in the host's dashboard as environment variables
3. Set `ALLOWED_ORIGIN` to your real portfolio URL once it's live, not `*`
4. Update `API_URL` in `assets/main.js` to the deployed URL and re-upload your site

## Notes

- Rate-limited to 5 submissions per 15 minutes per IP, to avoid abuse.
- Validates name/email/message server-side (not just in the browser).
- Sets `replyTo` to the sender's email, so hitting "Reply" in your inbox
  goes straight back to them.